<section class="band" id="forecast" aria-labelledby="forecast-heading">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="feature-intro">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Follow the snow</p><h2 id="forecast-heading">See the total.<br>Understand the timing.</h2></div>
      <p class="lede">A promising snow total is a starting point. Use the hourly forecast to see
        when snow is expected, then check the daily and weekly views to put it in context.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="weather-details">
      <article><h3>The next few hours</h3><p>Check current feels-like conditions and visibility,
        then follow hourly temperature, snowfall, and wind. Decide whether you want the first chair
        or a later window as conditions change.</p></article>
      <article><h3>The next few days</h3><p>Compare forecast snowfall over the next 24 hours,
        48 hours, and seven local days, including the current day at the resort. Daily outlooks help you follow the storm through the week
        instead of treating every day as a separate forecast.</p></article>
      <article><h3>The shape of a storm</h3><p>Storm timing highlights the expected start,
        peak, and snowfall periods.
        Follow how that outlook changes as new forecasts arrive.</p></article>
      <article><h3>A look at recent radar</h3><p>Step through recent RainViewer radar imagery
        to see precipitation around the mountain. This is a view of recent weather;
        the forecast provides the outlook for what comes next.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--tint" aria-labelledby="elevation-heading">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Weather at the elevation you ride</p>
    <h2 id="elevation-heading">Follow the forecast up the mountain</h2>
    <p class="lede">Switch between base, mid-mountain, and summit views. See the forecast
      for the part of the resort you&rsquo;re planning to ride. Available temperature,
      wind, and snow detail depends on the source.</p>
    <dl class="elevation-guide">
      <div><dt>Summit</dt><dd>Check temperatures, wind, and gusts where exposed terrain can change the feel of the day.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Mid-mountain</dt><dd>Explore the forecast for the middle of the resort and compare it with the top and base.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Base</dt><dd>Look at lower-mountain conditions alongside the precipitation outlook and freezing level where available.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="feature-note">Elevation views are forecast estimates. Some sources do not provide a distinct forecast for each elevation; these are not separate measurements at the base, middle, and summit.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band" aria-labelledby="signal-heading">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="feature-intro">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Your Ski Signal</p><h2 id="signal-heading">A recommendation<br>you can look into</h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Ski Signal brings the forecast together into a suggested riding window,
        explains the conditions behind it, and shows its confidence. Start there,
        then explore the details that matter to your day.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="weather-details">
      <article><h3>Plan a window, or a week</h3><p>The weather day planner helps compare
        riding windows. Week rankings help you spot promising days. Keep favorite resorts
        close and open each forecast as you decide where to go.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Get a feel for the snow</h3><p>Snow-quality estimates add context to
        accumulation totals. Look at the forecast alongside temperature and, where available,
        the freezing level to better understand what the mountain might feel like.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Give wind its own attention</h3><p>Check wind speed, gusts, and exposure
        alongside the snow forecast. Use the detail to weigh an exposed summit against
        other parts of your day.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Go deeper when you want to</h3><p>Optional advanced weather views add
        model comparisons and atmospheric detail where supported. Keep the everyday view
        simple, or open up more of the forecast behind it.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="feature-note">Ski Signal and day rankings describe forecast weather. They do not assess avalanche safety or confirm that terrain or lifts are open.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band weather-spotlight" aria-labelledby="alerts-heading">
  <div class="shell feature-intro">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Powder alerts, on your terms</p>
      <h2 id="alerts-heading">Your mountains.<br>Your kind of storm.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Set the conditions that get your attention, then let SkiBuds check
        the forecast against your preferences for favorite resorts.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <ul class="weather-checklist">
        <li><strong>Your snow threshold.</strong> Choose how much forecast snowfall qualifies.</li>
        <li><strong>Your wind limit.</strong> Add wind to the conditions you care about.</li>
        <li><strong>Your confidence setting.</strong> Set a minimum forecast confidence.</li>
        <li><strong>Your quiet hours.</strong> Choose when alerts should stay quiet.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="feature-note">Alerts are opt-in and evaluated when forecasts refresh and when
        you return to the app. Delivery depends on notification permissions and available forecast data.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band" aria-labelledby="sources-heading">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Know what&rsquo;s behind the outlook</p>
    <h2 id="sources-heading">Sources, freshness, and room for uncertainty</h2>
    <p class="lede">Weather changes. SkiBuds shows source information, update times,
      and confidence so you can understand the outlook you&rsquo;re using.</p>
    <div class="weather-details">
      <article><h3>Multiple weather sources</h3><p>The weather system brings together
        supported sources such as the U.S. National Weather Service, WeatherAPI, and
        MET Norway. Availability depends on the mountain, coverage, and configured services;
        the app identifies the sources behind the displayed forecast.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Observations alongside forecasts</h3><p>Where available, nearby SNOTEL
        stations add measured snow-depth context alongside the forecast. Forecast snowfall,
        observed conditions, and community lift or trail reports each have their own role
        in understanding the mountain.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Freshness you can check</h3><p>Update times and stale-data indicators
        help you see when information was refreshed. Check them again as a storm approaches
        or your plans change.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Detail that depends on coverage</h3><p>Forecast models, observations,
        radar, and advanced insights aren&rsquo;t available everywhere. SkiBuds surfaces
        the detail available for a location, so the depth of the weather view can vary
        between mountains.</p></article>
    </div>
    <a href="/faq/#mountain-weather" class="btn btn--quiet">Weather questions, answered &rarr;</a>
